    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d15">

                <pb facs="0092" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4731/132: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Sydney</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Moffat</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Sydney</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-09-26T15:00:00+10:00">September 26, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received September 26—5:14 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s September 23, 6 p.m. Proceeding to Canberra for week’s visit on
                    October 1st.</p>
                <p>The press here is no longer referring to the imminence of a trade treaty with the
                    United States. Neither Squire<anchor n="10" xml:id="d15-f1"/> nor I know of any
                    present plan to send a special delegation to the United States to negotiate a
                    reciprocal trade agreement though it is still expected that Gullett will stop
                    over in Washington on his way home from Europe to explore the situation in
                    greater detail than did Lyons. Gullett’s trade discussions with continental
                    countries have to date been unproductive.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Moffat</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d15-f1" n="10" xml:id="d15-f1-content">
                <p>E. C. Squire, American Trade Commissioner at Sydney.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
